# CURSES(3) [April 23, 1986]

## NAME

curses - screen functions with `optimal` cursor motion

## SYNOPSIS

| **cc** [ flags ] files **-lcurses -ltermcap** [ libraries ]

| Note: on this system that order is a contract, not a convention. *b6ld* scans each archive exactly once and in order, so **-lcurses** must precede **-ltermcap,** which must precede the implicit **-lc -lruntime.**

## DESCRIPTION

These routines give the user a method of updating screens with reasonable optimization. They
keep an image of the current screen, and the user sets up an image of a new one. Then the
*refresh()* tells the routines to make the current screen look like the new one. In order to
initialize the routines, the routine *initscr()* must be called before any of the other
routines that deal with windows and screens are used. The routine *endwin()* should be called
before exiting.

## BESM-6 NOTES

This is the 4.3BSD library, ported. See *lib/libcurses/README.md* in the source tree for the
whole account; what a caller needs to know is this.

*bool* is C11's *_Bool*, not a macro. 4.3BSD spelled the boolean capabilities and window flags
*bool*, which was its own macro for *int*; *<curses.h>* includes *<stdbool.h>* and declares
them with the real type instead. Redefining the name was not open to it - a *bool* macro of its
own would collide with the one *<stdbool.h>* already has, in either inclusion order. On this
machine *_Bool* occupies a whole word like every other scalar, so nothing changes size, and
converting any scalar to it is a zero test, so such an object never holds anything but 0 or 1.
*TRUE* and *FALSE* are *true* and *false*; *ERR* and *OK* are return codes and remain *int*.

The tty-mode routines - *raw()*, *noraw()*, *cbreak()*, *nocbreak()*, *echo()*, *noecho()*,
*nl()*, *nonl()*, *savetty()* and *resetty()* - are ioctl(2) calls on the console. Under the
user-level simulator *b6sim* every one of them succeeds and changes nothing, so a program whose
behaviour depends on the terminal really entering raw or cbreak mode must be run under the
booted kernel.

There is no job control, so nothing here catches SIGTSTP and there is no handler to reinstate
after a stop.

*longname()* and *fullname()* copy at most 49 characters into *def*, which is the size of the
*ttytype* buffer their only caller passes. 4.3BSD copied without a bound.

The terminal description is read from */etc/termcap*, or from **$TERMCAP** if that names an
absolute path or holds an entry outright.

